==English==
{{was wotd|2006|June|9}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|soliciten}}, {{m|enm|solliciten}}, from {{der|en|fro|soliciter}}, {{m|fro|solliciter}}, from {{der|en|la|sollicitāre||}}, present active participle of {{m|la|sollicitō|t=stir, disturb; look after}}, from {{m|la|sollicitus|t=agitated, anxious, punctilious|lit=thoroughly moved}}, from {{m|la|sollus|t=whole, entire}} + perfect passive participle of {{m|la|cieō|t=shake, excite, cite, to put in motion}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|sə-lĭs'ĭt}}
* {{IPA|en|/səˈlɪsɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-solicit.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-solicit.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪsɪt}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To persistently [[endeavor]] to obtain an object, or [[bring about]] an event.
#: ''to '''solicit''' alms, or a favour''
#* {{RQ:Pope Eloisa|passage=I view my crime, but kindle at the view, / Repent old pleasures, and '''solicit''' new.}}
#*: Did I '''solicit''' thee From darkness to promote me?
# To [[woo]]; to [[court]].
# To persuade or [[incite]] one to commit some act, especially illegal or sexual behavior.
#* {{RQ:Milton PL|8|passage=That fruit {{...}} '''sollicited''' her longing eye.}}
#* {{RQ:Locke Human}}, Book II, Chapter 1
#*: Sounds and some tangible qualities fail not to '''solicit''' their proper senses, and force an entrance to the mind.
#: ''If you want to lose your virginity, you should try to '''solicit''' some fine looking women.''
# To offer to perform sexual activity, especially when for a [[payment]].
#: ''My girlfriend tried to '''solicit''' me for sex, but I was tired.''
# To make a [[petition]].
# {{lb|en|archaic}} To [[disturb]] or trouble; to [[harass]].
# To urge the claims of; to [[plead]]; to act as [[solicitor]] for or with reference to.
#* '''1628''', {{w|John Ford (dramatist)|John Ford}}, ''{{w|The Lover's Melancholy}}''
#*: Should / My brother henceforth study to forget / The vow that he hath made thee, I would ever / '''Solicit''' thy deserts.
# {{lb|en|obsolete|rare}} To [[disturb]]; to [[disquiet]].
#* '''1611-1615''', {{w|George Chapman}}, ''{{w|Iliad}}'', Book XVI
#*: Hath any ill '''solicited''' thine ears?
#* {{RQ:Dryden Spanish Fryar|3|2}}
#*: But anxious fears '''solicit''' my weak breast.

